module {
import Types;
import Globals;
import Util;
import Load;
import Link;

{-
HLM is a high-level interpreter designed for execution on the ETH L1.
It is intended to run user metatransactions, store and execute virtual
smart contracts in Merkle storage and interact with the rest of the L1 on
the users' behalf.
Beyond providing gas savings through sharing
1)the fixed transaction cost,
2)storage access costs (via Merklization),
3)cold load costs (when interacting with external contracts)
and reducing the transaction authorization cost,
HLM aims to give L1 users access to a new functional programming paradigm
that will enable faster, more expressive and more composable programs, as well
as closer and safer cooperation.
-}

{-
Merklization is transparent to the user; the work of assembling Merkle proofs
and controlling Merkle layout is done by a sequencer.
Sequencing (whether centralized or via a PoS protocol) is necessary at least at
the per-Merkle tree level because modifications to a Merkle invalidate
subsequent proofs assuming the old state.
Fortunately, there is an upside: the need for planning by the sequencer
enables metatransactions to benefit from *hinting*, where the sequencer does
work off-chain and the user's script merely verifies it.
An example: sorting can be done in O(n) rather than O(log n) on-chain: the
sequencer hints a permutation, and the user checks it produces a sorted list.
A practical example: a user signs a metatransaction approving a token swap;
it releases A and attempts to withdraw B from the counterparty's balance
(reverting otherwise).
The user needn't specify the counterparty; that can be left to the sequencer.
Furthermore, require(someCond) in a metatransaction could be used to enforce
complex conditions without running the risk of the txn wasting gas against the
user's will (as is possible with ETH txns); ensuring the conditions are
fulfilled before submitting the txn is the responsibility of the sequencer.
Note hinting is essential to making Merkle accesses cheap - by placing data
sensibly (using off-chain planning), you can ensure correlated data has high
locality and hot data is placed high up.
My hope is that with good planning this will reduce the read-write cost of
data to approx 16 gas per byte (down from 156.25 + call overhead).

From the user's perspective, the logical state of the HLM is a set of objects
which reference each other. Objects are the basic unit of state considered by
the HLM; they include balances, mutable and immutable bytestrings and arrays,
closures and abstract datatypes.

To enable persistence, object references use 32b UIDs. Sequential allocation of
UIDs enables them to be much smaller than ETH addresses.

Calls to the HLM are divided into 5 phases:
1) Loading objects from Merkle storage,
2) Linking objects (replacing UIDs with memory pointers)
3) Execution (where user metatxns and virtual smart contracts run)
4) Unlinking
5) Hashing and storage
-}


main:()->();
main() := do {
 -- *******************Load phase********************************
 --Load phase params:
 --object pointer table size : uint16
 --object bytes len: uint16
 --object data: byte[len]

 --Initialize object pointer table, load object bytes into memory
 --TODO ensure the object table is null-terminated
 otblSize = *(0 :: CPtr Short);
 obytesLen = *(2 :: CPtr Short);
 obytes = (2 * otblSize) :: MPtr Byte;
 copy(obytes,4 :: CPtr Byte, obytesLen);
 --The load script is after the end of the object data
 loadScript = (obytesLen + 4) :: CPtr LoadInstr; --todo check for overflow
 
 --Load the objects into the object table
 ((pc,allocPtr,otblPtr),h) =
  load((loadScript,obytes :: OPtr,otable),otable+otblSize);
 require(h == merkleRoot);
 -- *******************Link phase********************************
 --Link phase params:
 --ixs, a concatenated list of lists of object table indices
 {-
 Algo:
 for obj in otable:      --until !obj :: OPtr
  for uidptr in obj:     --uidptr ranges over the ptrs to each uid in obj
   o = otable[pop(ixs)]
   if o.uid == *uidptr   --the UIDs need to match
   then *uidptr = o      --replace the UID with a memory pointer
   else revert()
   end
 -} 
 ixs = pc :: CPtr (UInt 16); --todo bound object table size...
 nextPtr = link(ixs)  --todo rename nextPtr to whatever it needs to be
 -- *******************Pre-execution phase***************************
 --Now we should have an object table containing linked preexisting
 --objects. But how do new objects get there? Through execution of metatxns.
 --Just as all commands in a REPL start as strings not containing any
 --object references, metatxns start as just a bytestring without any
 --special permissions (i.e. handles to objects).
 --That bytestring must be loaded into memory; any references to registered
 --objects (analogous to those available in the global env of a REPL) must be
 --resolved here.
 --Design:
 --s = init interpreter state
 --bs = alloc from calldata
 --os = parse ixs to registered objects, index otable
 --co = spawn(bs,os)
 --enter(s,co)
 --There's no need for a stack of entered coroutines in memory since
 --the EVM stack can be used (with the enter instruction performing a non-tail
 --call enter(s,co)). That's what Lua does IIRC. 
}
}